WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.477 --> 00:00:03.208
- It's so much in my memory

00:00:03.208 --> 00:00:04.971
the look on that little boy's face.

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.010
I think about that little boy

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:07.813
every single day working at Pixar.

00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:10.805
You have to put yourself our there.

00:00:10.805 --> 00:00:12.993
You have to be willing to fail.

00:00:12.993 --> 00:00:15.247
Even though it's never been done before

00:00:15.247 --> 00:00:16.574
doesn't mean it can't be done.

00:00:16.574 --> 00:00:18.847
Honestly, we put more
pressure on ourselves

00:00:18.847 --> 00:00:21.229
to make good movies for our audience

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:24.812
than any kind of pressure
from the outside.

00:00:26.108 --> 00:00:27.472
- He's an American animator,

00:00:27.472 --> 00:00:30.593
film director, and chief
creative office of Pixar

00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:33.360
and Walt Disney Animation Studios.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.388
He oversees all of Pixar's films

00:00:35.388 --> 00:00:38.042
and associated projects
as executive producer.

00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:41.153
He has an estimated net
worth of 100 million dollars.

00:00:41.153 --> 00:00:42.175
He's John Lasseter,

00:00:42.175 --> 00:00:45.129
and here's my take on his
top 10 rules of success.

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:46.860
Rule number one is my personal favorite,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.287
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:00:48.287 --> 00:00:49.753
for some special bonus clips.

00:00:49.753 --> 00:00:50.902
Also, as John is talking,

00:00:50.902 --> 00:00:53.146
if he says something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:53.146 --> 00:00:54.404
please leave it in the comments below

00:00:54.404 --> 00:00:55.434
and put quotes around it

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.987
so other people can be inspired, as well.

00:00:57.987 --> 00:00:58.820
Enjoy!

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:00.903
(swoosh)

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.510
(dramatic music)

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:15.689
- Everybody has their own
sense of accomplishment.

00:01:15.689 --> 00:01:17.473
Some it's opening weekend box office

00:01:17.473 --> 00:01:18.735
or winning Academy Awards.

00:01:18.735 --> 00:01:22.635
To me, it is being in
an audience anonymously

00:01:22.635 --> 00:01:24.304
and watching people watch my movies,

00:01:24.304 --> 00:01:27.750
and seeing how we're entertaining people.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:30.706
The first true experience
I ever had of that

00:01:30.706 --> 00:01:34.840
was about five days
after Toy Story came out.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.848
It took four years for me make Toy Story,

00:01:36.848 --> 00:01:41.015
all of us at Pixar, and I
was flying with my family.

00:01:42.077 --> 00:01:45.726
We were changing planes in the
Dallas Forth Worth Airport.

00:01:45.726 --> 00:01:47.907
I got off the plane and
there was a little boy

00:01:47.907 --> 00:01:50.395
about four years old with his mother,

00:01:50.395 --> 00:01:54.761
and he was holding a
Woody the Cowboy doll,

00:01:54.761 --> 00:01:55.816
and it's the first time I'd seen

00:01:55.816 --> 00:02:00.035
one of my characters outside
of Pixar, and I was so stunned.

00:02:00.035 --> 00:02:02.106
My boy, "Dad, look, look, look!"

00:02:02.106 --> 00:02:04.087
The look on his face,

00:02:04.087 --> 00:02:06.202
he couldn't wait to show his father

00:02:06.202 --> 00:02:09.748
who was getting off the plane his toy,

00:02:09.748 --> 00:02:11.134
and I realized at that moment

00:02:11.134 --> 00:02:13.384
that that character that I,

00:02:14.227 --> 00:02:15.471
with the help of everybody at Pixar,

00:02:15.471 --> 00:02:18.964
created over four years,
no longer belonged to me,

00:02:18.964 --> 00:02:20.547
it belonged to him.

00:02:21.668 --> 00:02:24.918
To be honest, it's so much in my memory

00:02:26.931 --> 00:02:28.440
the look on that little boy's face,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:29.591
I think about that little boy

00:02:29.591 --> 00:02:31.292
every single day working at Pixar,

00:02:31.292 --> 00:02:33.253
and that's why we do what we do.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:36.303
To create something that
connects with people,

00:02:36.303 --> 00:02:38.142
that moves people,

00:02:38.142 --> 00:02:42.637
is the most fulfilling and
satisfying work there is.

00:02:42.637 --> 00:02:46.205
For an artist, it is truly a privilege

00:02:46.205 --> 00:02:48.761
to do this for a living.

00:02:48.761 --> 00:02:51.156
But with that privilege
comes responsibility,

00:02:51.156 --> 00:02:55.232
and the privilege and
responsibility are one in the same,

00:02:55.232 --> 00:02:56.899
to do something new.

00:02:59.983 --> 00:03:02.733
I say that it is a responsibility

00:03:03.607 --> 00:03:07.198
because to really truly be creative,

00:03:07.198 --> 00:03:10.667
you have to be willing to take risks.

00:03:10.667 --> 00:03:13.729
You have to put yourself out there.

00:03:13.729 --> 00:03:16.644
You have to be willing to fail.

00:03:16.644 --> 00:03:20.146
Failure has such a negative
connotation in our society,

00:03:20.146 --> 00:03:21.695
and I think this totally unfair,

00:03:21.695 --> 00:03:25.862
because what failure really is
is the opportunity to learn.

00:03:27.203 --> 00:03:29.182
If we never failed,

00:03:29.182 --> 00:03:32.738
it would never occur to us
to do thing differently.

00:03:32.738 --> 00:03:35.303
You would just keep doing the same thing

00:03:35.303 --> 00:03:37.513
because it would always work.

00:03:37.513 --> 00:03:39.866
But the world changes,

00:03:39.866 --> 00:03:43.199
people change, and so we have to change.

00:03:44.518 --> 00:03:47.069
We have to fail in order to succeed.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:49.259
I think the biggest thing for us

00:03:49.259 --> 00:03:51.716
is that we studied films.

00:03:51.716 --> 00:03:53.577
We watched films religiously.

00:03:53.577 --> 00:03:55.796
We were making...

00:03:55.796 --> 00:03:58.190
With Toy Story, it was a buddy picture.

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:01.409
We watched every buddy
picture we could find

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:02.931
and analyzed it.

00:04:02.931 --> 00:04:06.030
Good ones, and it's very
important to watch bad ones, too.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.277
No seriously, it is very important.

00:04:08.277 --> 00:04:10.786
You watch a bunch of good
ones and watch a few bad ones,

00:04:10.786 --> 00:04:12.812
and pretty soon you'll start seeing,

00:04:12.812 --> 00:04:16.062
"Huh, this one, defiant ones did this,"

00:04:17.908 --> 00:04:18.803
I'm not going to say it,

00:04:18.803 --> 00:04:21.502
"These other films did that,

00:04:21.502 --> 00:04:23.541
"and that's why it doesn't quite work.

00:04:23.541 --> 00:04:24.979
"Interesting, okay."

00:04:24.979 --> 00:04:28.024
And you start understanding
what they did and more and more.

00:04:28.024 --> 00:04:30.374
And don't copy things,

00:04:30.374 --> 00:04:32.469
it's about understanding and learning.

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:36.636
Be thirsty for knowledge,
be thirsty for knowledge.

00:04:37.903 --> 00:04:40.930
It will always make your work better.

00:04:40.930 --> 00:04:44.278
I believe so strongly that
no matter what you do,

00:04:44.278 --> 00:04:47.750
create the best quality
you can in whatever you do.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:49.982
Quality is the best business plan.

00:04:49.982 --> 00:04:52.424
It's the only business
plan I've ever lived.

00:04:52.424 --> 00:04:55.757
Pixar, Up is going to be our 10th movie,

00:04:57.178 --> 00:04:59.678
and it's a record in Hollywood

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:05.486
that's unlike any record
that anyone has ever seen,

00:05:05.486 --> 00:05:07.319
10 successes in a row.

00:05:08.699 --> 00:05:10.159
So quality is the best business plan.

00:05:10.159 --> 00:05:13.550
And we surround ourselves with
absolutely the best people,

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:15.674
and I believe very strongly in this.

00:05:15.674 --> 00:05:17.386
So often you will find yourself

00:05:17.386 --> 00:05:21.219
in a situation where there is a hierarchy,

00:05:21.219 --> 00:05:25.386
that ideas are important
because of who is saying them,

00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:31.924
because they have a
higher position than you.

00:05:31.924 --> 00:05:32.911
I believe very strongly

00:05:32.911 --> 00:05:35.828
it doesn't matter whose idea it is.

00:05:36.665 --> 00:05:39.821
The best idea at Pixar we always use.

00:05:39.821 --> 00:05:41.739
The ideas come from the receptionist,

00:05:41.739 --> 00:05:43.294
they come from the cooks,

00:05:43.294 --> 00:05:46.455
they come from everybody, and we use them,

00:05:46.455 --> 00:05:50.622
because everybody is there
to make the movies better.

00:05:51.671 --> 00:05:54.829
Our people are so good right now

00:05:54.829 --> 00:05:56.313
and they're so accomplished,

00:05:56.313 --> 00:05:59.396
and our technology has grown so much,

00:06:00.855 --> 00:06:03.030
and that's what Pixar is about.

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:06.398
Everyone at Pixar lives for that day

00:06:06.398 --> 00:06:07.815
that will come to them and say,

00:06:07.815 --> 00:06:09.699
"We don't know how to do this.

00:06:09.699 --> 00:06:12.116
"Here, try to figure it out."

00:06:13.445 --> 00:06:15.047
They live for that,

00:06:15.047 --> 00:06:18.319
'cause what's interesting about Pixar,

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:22.728
and I think it's really a
product of Northern California,

00:06:22.728 --> 00:06:26.295
Silicon Valley, Steve Jobs, George Lucas,

00:06:26.295 --> 00:06:27.962
it's all this belief

00:06:29.539 --> 00:06:33.206
that even though it's
never been done before

00:06:33.206 --> 00:06:35.445
doesn't mean it can't be done, right?

00:06:35.445 --> 00:06:39.612
There's no one here that
says, "No, it can't be done."

00:06:42.254 --> 00:06:43.929
Every movie we've made,

00:06:43.929 --> 00:06:47.596
there's aspects of it
that's unlike anything

00:06:49.017 --> 00:06:52.508
you've ever seen before, even the sequels.

00:06:52.508 --> 00:06:57.321
You look Toy Story, and Toy
Story 2, and Toy Story 3,

00:06:57.321 --> 00:06:59.834
and I tell you, there's things
in both of those sequels

00:06:59.834 --> 00:07:03.039
that are unlike anything
that's been done before it.

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:04.518
And I think definitely looking at

00:07:04.518 --> 00:07:07.101
Cars and Cars 2 is so different

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:13.027
in kind of the look and feel.

00:07:13.027 --> 00:07:14.777
Mark Twain once said,

00:07:16.746 --> 00:07:19.942
"The two most important days of your life

00:07:19.942 --> 00:07:21.879
"are the day you were born

00:07:21.879 --> 00:07:24.462
"and the day you find out why."

00:07:25.454 --> 00:07:27.434
The first of those has
already happened to you

00:07:27.434 --> 00:07:30.948
'cause you're sitting right here, okay?

00:07:30.948 --> 00:07:33.577
The second, they day that you find out

00:07:33.577 --> 00:07:38.545
why you were born, may have
already happened to many of you,

00:07:38.545 --> 00:07:40.820
and to the others, not yet,

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.847
but you will know when it happens.

00:07:43.847 --> 00:07:48.148
It will be connected to
something that you truly love.

00:07:48.148 --> 00:07:51.101
I've always said to my five sons,

00:07:51.101 --> 00:07:53.892
"Choose something that you love to do

00:07:53.892 --> 00:07:56.422
"and you'll never work
a day in your life."

00:07:56.422 --> 00:07:59.758
Is that every time you do
something for the first time,

00:07:59.758 --> 00:08:03.591
a paper, a movie, a
story, a script, whatever,

00:08:05.632 --> 00:08:07.955
chances are the first
time, the first pass,

00:08:07.955 --> 00:08:09.705
is going to be wrong.

00:08:11.731 --> 00:08:13.572
So Andrew Stanton coined this phrase,

00:08:13.572 --> 00:08:14.695
and we use it all the time at Pixar,

00:08:14.695 --> 00:08:16.784
"Be wrong as fast as you can."

00:08:16.784 --> 00:08:17.617
Get it up there.

00:08:17.617 --> 00:08:19.606
Be wrong as fast as you can,

00:08:19.606 --> 00:08:22.503
because then with honesty around you

00:08:22.503 --> 00:08:25.265
you will get to being right much quicker.

00:08:25.265 --> 00:08:29.585
Very, very, very important,
do not work in a vacuum.

00:08:29.585 --> 00:08:31.585
Do not work in a vacuum.

00:08:32.471 --> 00:08:35.357
You may be the filmmaker
and doing something,

00:08:35.357 --> 00:08:39.088
but you have to surround
yourself with trusted people.

00:08:39.088 --> 00:08:43.470
You have to, you get so
immersed in your work,

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:44.851
you will, everybody does,

00:08:44.851 --> 00:08:46.423
you get so immersed in your work,

00:08:46.423 --> 00:08:50.627
I guarantee you you
will not be able to see

00:08:50.627 --> 00:08:53.190
the forest from the trees.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:55.279
Frankly, you'll be
studying the pine needles

00:08:55.279 --> 00:08:56.501
and worrying about them,

00:08:56.501 --> 00:08:58.159
and you need someone to help you back up

00:08:58.159 --> 00:09:00.058
and take a look at the forest

00:09:00.058 --> 00:09:03.694
and see where things are
working or not working.

00:09:03.694 --> 00:09:07.623
And you need to surround
yourself with people

00:09:07.623 --> 00:09:09.706
whose judgment you trust,

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:15.543
and they can be brutally honest with you.

00:09:15.543 --> 00:09:16.527
And you have to be able to...

00:09:16.527 --> 00:09:18.339
Look, as an artist,

00:09:18.339 --> 00:09:22.506
showing unfinished work to
people is really difficult,

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.805
is really hard, it always is hard,

00:09:25.805 --> 00:09:28.561
it always will be hard,
it never gets any easier,

00:09:28.561 --> 00:09:29.943
but you have to do it.

00:09:29.943 --> 00:09:31.693
You know, it's funny.

00:09:33.026 --> 00:09:37.769
(mumbles) asks, "How do
you cope with the pressure?

00:09:37.769 --> 00:09:38.975
"Do you feel the pressure?"

00:09:38.975 --> 00:09:41.363
Honestly, we put more
pressure on ourselves

00:09:41.363 --> 00:09:43.787
to make good movies for our audience

00:09:43.787 --> 00:09:47.370
than any kind of pressure
from the outside.

00:09:50.146 --> 00:09:51.822
It was very important for me

00:09:51.822 --> 00:09:54.739
when Bob Iger and Steve Jobs agreed

00:09:56.060 --> 00:10:00.254
to let Disney buy Pixar and
merge the two companies,

00:10:00.254 --> 00:10:01.376
and they asked me to become

00:10:01.376 --> 00:10:04.157
chief creative officer
of Disney Animation,

00:10:04.157 --> 00:10:07.991
as well as continue doing
that with Pixar Animation.

00:10:07.991 --> 00:10:10.363
And I came home to Disney,

00:10:10.363 --> 00:10:11.783
and it's like coming home, really,

00:10:11.783 --> 00:10:15.193
'cause I've always done things,

00:10:15.193 --> 00:10:17.693
I've always wanted to work for
Walt Disney when I was a kid

00:10:17.693 --> 00:10:19.075
and be a Disney animator,

00:10:19.075 --> 00:10:21.974
and I loved everything about Disney.

00:10:21.974 --> 00:10:23.052
And even at Pixar,

00:10:23.052 --> 00:10:25.701
I made movies in the style of Walt Disney,

00:10:25.701 --> 00:10:28.284
and coming home to Disney to me

00:10:29.616 --> 00:10:33.783
the goal was to make the
studio make great movies again

00:10:34.988 --> 00:10:38.373
to truly deeply entertain audiences,

00:10:38.373 --> 00:10:41.158
to put the filmmakers, the artists,

00:10:41.158 --> 00:10:43.232
the animators, the story people,

00:10:43.232 --> 00:10:47.145
kind of creatively in
charge of these films again,

00:10:47.145 --> 00:10:50.911
not let these executives
tell them what to do,

00:10:50.911 --> 00:10:53.575
let the artists, the filmmakers,

00:10:53.575 --> 00:10:56.286
it's why they're there,

00:10:56.286 --> 00:10:59.750
but to challenge them to
make these films great,

00:10:59.750 --> 00:11:01.055
and to me I will never...

00:11:01.055 --> 00:11:03.844
You know it takes four years
to make an animated film,

00:11:03.844 --> 00:11:07.245
and you have every chance,
this is what I say,

00:11:07.245 --> 00:11:10.509
you have every chance to do it right.

00:11:10.509 --> 00:11:12.256
And I am so dedicated

00:11:12.256 --> 00:11:15.441
to making sure that it's
good movies out there,

00:11:15.441 --> 00:11:18.469
and that's why we have
a good track record,

00:11:18.469 --> 00:11:19.814
because we have this belief

00:11:19.814 --> 00:11:23.250
that we can do it and we will do it.

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:26.167
In your life, you will start a job,

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:28.153
and this is what I say

00:11:28.153 --> 00:11:31.349
to every single new
person coming into Pixar.

00:11:31.349 --> 00:11:33.223
No matter what you do,

00:11:33.223 --> 00:11:37.675
make yourself indispensable
to that organization.

00:11:37.675 --> 00:11:40.245
Make yourself indispensable,

00:11:40.245 --> 00:11:42.587
where people after you're
done with your job,

00:11:42.587 --> 00:11:46.683
people cannot imagine
the place without you,

00:11:46.683 --> 00:11:50.850
and without asking for a
thing, your career will be set.

00:11:53.684 --> 00:11:55.276
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:55.276 --> 00:11:58.321
I made this video because
Chaitan Joshi asked me to,

00:11:58.321 --> 00:11:59.368
so if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:11:59.368 --> 00:12:01.143
that you want me to profile next,

00:12:01.143 --> 00:12:02.484
leave it down in the comments below

00:12:02.484 --> 00:12:03.716
and I'll see what I can do.

00:12:03.716 --> 00:12:05.186
I'd also love to know which of the rules

00:12:05.186 --> 00:12:07.426
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:12:07.426 --> 00:12:09.976
What change are you
going to immediately make

00:12:09.976 --> 00:12:12.881
to your life or business
after watching this video?

00:12:12.881 --> 00:12:14.095
Please leave it in the comments.

00:12:14.095 --> 00:12:15.654
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:12:15.654 --> 00:12:17.654
Finally want to give a quick
shout out to Kevin Lee.

00:12:17.654 --> 00:12:18.702
Kevin, thank you so much

00:12:18.702 --> 00:12:20.375
for picking up a copy of my book,

00:12:20.375 --> 00:12:21.948
coming in to get it autographed

00:12:21.948 --> 00:12:23.557
and then posted on Facebook.

00:12:23.557 --> 00:12:25.147
I really, really, really appreciate

00:12:25.147 --> 00:12:26.647
the support and the love.

00:12:26.647 --> 00:12:27.985
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:12:27.985 --> 00:12:29.219
I believe in you.

00:12:29.219 --> 00:12:31.389
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:12:31.389 --> 00:12:33.800
and whatever your one word is.

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:35.245
Much love.

00:12:35.245 --> 00:12:36.434
I'll see you soon.

00:12:36.434 --> 00:12:38.517
(swoosh)

00:12:42.499 --> 00:12:43.576
- You know one of the exciting things

00:12:43.576 --> 00:12:46.001
about the merger between Disney and Pixar,

00:12:46.001 --> 00:12:47.373
and Bob Iger making me

00:12:47.373 --> 00:12:49.559
chief creative officer
and Catmull president

00:12:49.559 --> 00:12:53.194
of the Walt Disney Animation Studio

00:12:53.194 --> 00:12:55.486
was that the first thing
that we wanted to do

00:12:55.486 --> 00:12:57.841
when we came into the studio

00:12:57.841 --> 00:13:00.169
is we wanted to start production

00:13:00.169 --> 00:13:02.738
on a hand drawn animated film.

00:13:02.738 --> 00:13:05.622
I love the medium of hand drawn animation.

00:13:05.622 --> 00:13:06.455
I love it.

00:13:06.455 --> 00:13:07.828
I think it's fantastic,

00:13:07.828 --> 00:13:11.995
and I never quite understood
why animation studios,

00:13:13.107 --> 00:13:16.828
like all around, especially in Hollywood,

00:13:16.828 --> 00:13:17.661
but all around the world,

00:13:17.661 --> 00:13:20.040
were deciding that audiences

00:13:20.040 --> 00:13:24.162
weren't interested in watching
hand drawn animation anymore.

00:13:24.162 --> 00:13:25.990
The way I look at it is like

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.454
think of two movie studios

00:13:28.454 --> 00:13:30.544
that are making live action films.

00:13:30.544 --> 00:13:31.846
One makes really popular films

00:13:31.846 --> 00:13:34.868
and the other one just so so films.

00:13:34.868 --> 00:13:38.129
The so so films studio would
kind of look over and go,

00:13:38.129 --> 00:13:39.296
"Oh, I get it.

00:13:40.190 --> 00:13:42.386
"I know why our films are not
doing well at the box office.

00:13:42.386 --> 00:13:45.011
"We're using the wrong camera.

00:13:45.011 --> 00:13:47.670
"We're going to use that
camera where that company

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:51.033
"its films studio is doing amazing work,

00:13:51.033 --> 00:13:54.537
"and now our movies are going
to be liked by audiences."

00:13:54.537 --> 00:13:56.338
Now of course that's ridiculous,

00:13:56.338 --> 00:13:58.797
but that's the way we feel
about hand drawn animation

00:13:58.797 --> 00:14:00.549
versus computer animation.

00:14:00.549 --> 00:14:02.751
Never in the history of cinema

00:14:02.751 --> 00:14:05.772
has the movie been
entertaining to an audience

00:14:05.772 --> 00:14:07.939
because of the technology.

00:14:09.092 --> 00:14:10.678
It is what you do with the technology,

00:14:10.678 --> 00:14:13.930
it's what you do with the
medium, it's so special.

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:16.281
Andrew Stanton said this,

00:14:16.281 --> 00:14:18.969
and I think it's a perfect sentiment,

00:14:18.969 --> 00:14:21.517
is that 2D animation became the scapegoat

00:14:21.517 --> 00:14:23.569
for bad storytelling.

00:14:23.569 --> 00:14:26.240
What the audiences don't
want to watch is bad movies.

00:14:26.240 --> 00:14:28.134
They like watching good movies.

00:14:28.134 --> 00:14:30.281
The thing I always tell

00:14:30.281 --> 00:14:34.806
students that really want
to work in animation,

00:14:34.806 --> 00:14:36.947
or work in film for that matter,

00:14:36.947 --> 00:14:40.030
is do not forget to study the basics.

00:14:43.048 --> 00:14:45.987
Basic drawing, even for computer animation

00:14:45.987 --> 00:14:47.569
you should know how to draw,

00:14:47.569 --> 00:14:51.319
basic design, basic
fundamentals of animation

00:14:52.802 --> 00:14:55.641
where you learn the
principles of animation,

00:14:55.641 --> 00:14:58.808
film grammar, how do you stage things,

00:15:01.291 --> 00:15:05.106
you know, how they do it
in live action filmmaking,

00:15:05.106 --> 00:15:06.356
story, writing,

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:11.815
three act story structure
is so vital to this.

00:15:11.815 --> 00:15:14.303
You got to learn all these basics,

00:15:14.303 --> 00:15:18.380
because this is kind of
like eating vegetables.

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:19.401
A lot of people don't want to do it

00:15:19.401 --> 00:15:21.848
and they just get onto
the more flashy stuff

00:15:21.848 --> 00:15:24.860
of using all the latest software,

00:15:24.860 --> 00:15:25.708
but the thing is

00:15:25.708 --> 00:15:30.407
is through your career
the software will change.

00:15:30.407 --> 00:15:32.874
It will always evolve and get better.

00:15:32.874 --> 00:15:34.859
What's important, and remember this,

00:15:34.859 --> 00:15:38.334
software never makes a movie entertaining,

00:15:38.334 --> 00:15:39.980
it's what you do with the software,

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:42.730
and what you do with the software

00:15:44.714 --> 00:15:49.088
you'll learn that from
the basic fundamentals.

00:15:49.088 --> 00:15:51.755
I rely on the basic fundamentals

00:15:54.269 --> 00:15:56.565
of art and design and filmmaking

00:15:56.565 --> 00:15:59.480
and animation and storytelling

00:15:59.480 --> 00:16:01.370
every single day of my career.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:03.023
It's something that just is a part of you.

00:16:03.023 --> 00:16:05.582
It's the foundation in which you work,

00:16:05.582 --> 00:16:08.807
and without those, you won't go anywhere,

00:16:08.807 --> 00:16:11.301
so it's really important to
get the fundamentals down.

00:16:11.301 --> 00:16:13.593
I loved animation.

00:16:13.593 --> 00:16:16.791
I knew it was not just for kids.

00:16:16.791 --> 00:16:20.619
Walt Disney never made
his movies just for kids.

00:16:20.619 --> 00:16:23.192
He made them for everybody.

00:16:23.192 --> 00:16:27.732
Chuck Jones made his
cartoons to show in theaters

00:16:27.732 --> 00:16:31.671
in front of Warner
Brothers movies for adults,

00:16:31.671 --> 00:16:35.838
but I felt it had to be
done for today's audience,

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:40.075
it had to be done in a new way.

00:16:40.075 --> 00:16:42.253
I found the answer when
I was working at Disney,

00:16:42.253 --> 00:16:43.964
computer animation.

00:16:43.964 --> 00:16:44.797
I knew this was it.

00:16:44.797 --> 00:16:47.739
I knew this was the future.

00:16:47.739 --> 00:16:49.334
You see, one of the things

00:16:49.334 --> 00:16:51.637
that I always admired about Walt Disney

00:16:51.637 --> 00:16:53.804
was his innovative spirit.

00:16:56.157 --> 00:16:59.657
Walt had been a great advocate of the new,

00:17:01.022 --> 00:17:03.984
and many groundbreaking
developments of film technology

00:17:03.984 --> 00:17:06.266
had their origins at his studio.

00:17:06.266 --> 00:17:09.568
I threw myself into suggesting projects

00:17:09.568 --> 00:17:12.261
that would show how the computer

00:17:12.261 --> 00:17:16.782
could be used to take Disney
animation to the next level.

00:17:16.782 --> 00:17:18.282
I even did a test.

00:17:19.923 --> 00:17:24.521
I kept getting the answers,
"No, that's not how it's done.

00:17:24.521 --> 00:17:25.354
"Is it cheaper?

00:17:25.354 --> 00:17:26.614
"Is it faster?

00:17:26.614 --> 00:17:29.614
"Then no, just do what you're told."

00:17:31.569 --> 00:17:34.683
No one was willing to take risks,

00:17:34.683 --> 00:17:35.573
but that didn't stop me.

00:17:35.573 --> 00:17:36.740
I kept trying.

00:17:37.774 --> 00:17:40.891
I just couldn't let go of the idea

00:17:40.891 --> 00:17:42.482
that computer animation was something

00:17:42.482 --> 00:17:44.197
Disney should be doing,

00:17:44.197 --> 00:17:45.814
because I knew it would make the studio

00:17:45.814 --> 00:17:47.556
and its films better.

00:17:47.556 --> 00:17:51.059
Then one day the manager of
the animation department,

00:17:51.059 --> 00:17:55.226
Mister No himself, called me
into his office and fired me.

00:17:59.495 --> 00:18:03.119
To be fired from the place of your dreams

00:18:03.119 --> 00:18:06.383
was so painful and embarrassing for me

00:18:06.383 --> 00:18:07.543
that it took decades

00:18:07.543 --> 00:18:10.646
before I could actually
tell people about it.

00:18:10.646 --> 00:18:12.450
I made this kind of excuse up

00:18:12.450 --> 00:18:16.223
that I followed my dreams somewhere else.

00:18:16.223 --> 00:18:18.223
Even as I got married,

00:18:18.223 --> 00:18:21.273
it took me a long time
to tell Nancy, my wife,

00:18:21.273 --> 00:18:23.191
I had actually gotten fired.

00:18:23.191 --> 00:18:25.002
But as fate would have it,

00:18:25.002 --> 00:18:28.868
I met Ed Catmull of the
Lucasfilm Computer Division.

00:18:28.868 --> 00:18:32.136
He and his group were doing
the most innovative research

00:18:32.136 --> 00:18:34.557
in the world on computer graphics,

00:18:34.557 --> 00:18:38.576
and unlike the Disney that just fired me,

00:18:38.576 --> 00:18:43.071
Ed was so excited about
what I was trying to do,

00:18:43.071 --> 00:18:45.667
to combine classic animation principles

00:18:45.667 --> 00:18:47.956
to computer animation.

00:18:47.956 --> 00:18:49.297
I finally found someone

00:18:49.297 --> 00:18:53.012
who could see the future as I saw it.

00:18:53.012 --> 00:18:56.704
He invited me to come work with him.

00:18:56.704 --> 00:19:00.717
Then in 1986, Steve
Jobs entered our lives.

00:19:00.717 --> 00:19:04.800
He was so excited about
what we were trying to do

00:19:06.292 --> 00:19:08.633
that he bought our group from Lucasfilm,

00:19:08.633 --> 00:19:12.050
and we formed a new company called Pixar.

00:19:12.930 --> 00:19:14.588
He really pushed us to do

00:19:14.588 --> 00:19:16.729
what no one else had ever done before.

00:19:16.729 --> 00:19:18.158
The result?

00:19:18.158 --> 00:19:20.523
In 1995, we released the first

00:19:20.523 --> 00:19:22.851
computer animated feature film ever made.

00:19:22.851 --> 00:19:24.684
We released Toy Story.

00:19:25.701 --> 00:19:27.951
(applause)

